    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the greatest common factor (GCF) of 40 and 48, we can use the prime factorization method.\n\n1. **Prime Factorization:**\n\n   - First, we find the prime factorization of 40:\n     \\[\n     40 \\div 2 = 20 \\\\\n     20 \\div 2 = 10 \\\\\n     10 \\div 2 = 5 \\\\\n     5 \\div 5 = 1\n     \\]\n     So, the prime factorization of 40 is:\n     \\[\n     40 = 2^3 \\times 5\n     \\]\n\n   - Next, we find the prime factorization of 48:\n     \\[\n     48 \\div 2 = 24 \\\\\n     24 \\div 2 = 12 \\\\\n     12 \\div 2 = 6 \\\\\n     6 \\div 2 = 3 \\\\\n     3 \\div 3 = 1\n     \\]\n     So, the prime factorization of 48 is:\n     \\[\n     48 = 2^4 \\times 3\n     \\]\n\n2. **Identify Common Prime Factors:**\n\n   - The common prime factors of 40 and 48 are the primes that appear in both factorizations. Here, the only common prime factor is 2.\n\n3. **Determine the Smallest Power of the Common Prime Factor:**\n\n   - The smallest power of 2 that appears in both factorizations is \\(2^3\\).\n   \n4. **Calculate the GCF:**\n\n   - The GCF is obtained by multiplying the smallest powers of all common prime factors:\n     \\[\n     \\text{GCF} = 2^3 = 8\n     \\]\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of 40 and 48 is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
