      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the greatest common factor (GCF) of 40 and 48, we need to determine the largest number that divides both 40 and 48 without leaving a remainder.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of 40 and 48:\n\n1. **Prime Factorization of 40:**\n   - 40 is divisible by 2:\n     \\[\n     40 \\div 2 = 20\n     \\]\n     So, \\( 40 = 2 \\times 20 \\).\n   - 20 is divisible by 2:\n     \\[\n     20 \\div 2 = 10\n     \\]\n     So, \\( 20 = 2 \\times 10 \\).\n   - 10 is divisible by 2:\n     \\[\n     10 \\div 2 = 5\n     \\]\n     So, \\( 10 = 2 \\times 5 \\).\n   - 5 is a prime number.\n     \\[\n     5 \\div 5 = 1\n     \\]\n     So, \\( 5 = 5 \\).\n\n   Therefore, the prime factorization of 40 is:\n   \\[\n   40 = 2^3 \\times 5\n   \\]\n\n2. **Prime Factorization of 48:**\n   - 48 is divisible by 2:\n     \\[\n     48 \\div 2 = 24\n     \\]\n     So, \\( 48 = 2 \\times 24 \\).\n   - 24 is divisible by 2:\n     \\[\n     24 \\div 2 = 12\n     \\]\n     So, \\( 24 = 2 \\times 12 \\).\n   - 12 is divisible by 2:\n     \\[\n     12 \\div 2 = 6\n     \\]\n     So, \\( 12 = 2 \\times 6 \\).\n   - 6 is divisible by 2:\n     \\[\n     6 \\div 2 = 3\n     \\]\n     So, \\( 6 = 2 \\times 3 \\).\n   - 3 is a prime number.\n     \\[\n     3 \\div 3 = 1\n     \\]\n     So, \\( 3 = 3 \\).\n\n   Therefore, the prime factorization of 48 is:\n   \\[\n   48 = 2^4 \\times 3\n   \\]\n\nNext, we identify the common prime factors and their lowest powers in both factorizations:\n- The common prime factor is 2.\n- The lowest power of 2 common to both factorizations is \\( 2^3 \\).\n\nThus, the greatest common factor (GCF) of 40 and 48 is:\n\\[\n2^3 = 8\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the greatest common factor of 40 and 48 is \\boxed{8}.",
